 ],
 "literal": "Having given birth and nurturing is my foremost duty. / Making him a noble man is the father's duty. / Forging and giving a spear is the blacksmith's duty. / Granting good conduct is the king's duty. / Destroying the difficult battle with a shining sword, / slaying the war-elephant and returning is the young man's duty.",
 "translation": "To give birth and nurture him is my foremost duty. To make him a noble man of character is his father's duty. To forge and give him a sharp spear is the blacksmith's duty. To guide him in righteous conduct is the king's duty. To destroy the enemy in fierce battle with a shining sword, slay their war-elephant, and return victorious is the young man's duty.",
 "modern": "பெற்று வளர்ப்பது என் தலையாய கடமையாகும். அவனைச் சான்றோனாக ஆக்குவது தந்தையின் கடமையாகும். அவனுக்கு வேல் வடித்துக் கொடுப்பது கொல்லனின் கடமையாகும். அவனுக்கு நன்னடத்தையை வழங்குவது அரசனின் கடமையாகும். ஒளிரும் வாளோடு கடினமான போர்க்களத்தில் பகைவரை அழித்து, யானையைக் கொன்று வெற்றி பெற்றுத் திரும்புவது காளையாகிய இளைஞனின் கடமையாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This famous poem from the Purananuru anthology is attributed to the poet Ponmudiyar, speaking in the voice of a mother or wise elder. It outlines the interconnected social and personal duties of different individuals in a heroic society. The poem systematically divides responsibilities: the mother's duty is to birth and nurture, the father's is to educate, the blacksmith's is to arm, the king's is to civilize, and finally, the young warrior's duty is to fight bravely and return victorious. The imagery of the 'shining sword' and 'slaying the war-elephant' represents the ultimate test of manhood and bravery in the Puram landscape. The key word 'katane' (duty) is repeated at the end of almost every line, emphasizing the absolute nature of these societal roles.",
